course: history
lesson: 42
title: Volodymyr, Yaroslav the Wise and the Mongols
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Around 988 Prince Volodymyr of Kyiv took Christianity from Byzantium; Danylo says he dunked everyone in the Dnipro in one day, but a whole land took generations to change, the way a school takes years to change its habits, not one assembly.
explain: Volodymyr was not a saint from the start: as a young pagan prince he fought his own brothers for the throne, and only later built churches and schools; history keeps both halves of a life.
explain: His son Yaroslav the Wise built Saint Sophia, gathered books, began the law code Ruska Pravda and married his daughters to kings, so Anna of Kyiv became Queen of France; Danylo calls him «father-in-law of all Europe», which is too much, but three royal marriages are still remarkable.
explain: After Yaroslav his heirs quarrelled and Rus broke into rival princedoms, and in 1240 the Mongols took Kyiv; the teacher said it was «wiped off the map», but Kyiv survived as a smaller town, and in the west the kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia carried on.
explain: The wise lesson: quarrels at home weaken a state before enemies arrive, and an empire like the Mongols can be cruel in conquest and still good for trade, both at once.
fact: Prince Volodymyr accepted Christianity from Byzantium around 988; the chronicle tells of a mass baptism of Kyivans in the river, and the new faith then spread slowly over generations, mixing with old beliefs.
fact: The chronicle says Volodymyr heard envoys of Islam, Judaism and Western and Byzantine Christianity, and his envoys praised Constantinople's churches; the chronicle even jokes that he turned Islam down because it forbade drinking; the real reasons were mostly politics, trade and a marriage alliance with Byzantium.
fact: Before his baptism Volodymyr was a fierce pagan prince who fought his brothers for the throne; afterwards he built churches and schools, and the Church later made him a saint.
fact: Yaroslav the Wise ruled from 1019 to 1054, built Saint Sophia and gathered books; his daughters married kings: Anna to Henry I of France, Elisabeth to Harald of Norway and Anastasia to Andrew I of Hungary.
fact: Anna Yaroslavna became Queen of France in 1051; a document survives with her signature in Cyrillic letters, but there is no proof that her husband could not read.
fact: The Ruska Pravda, begun under Yaroslav, is one of the earliest written law codes of the eastern Slavs; it replaced blood revenge with fines for many crimes, though Roman and Anglo-Saxon laws were older.
fact: After Yaroslav died, his sons and grandsons fought each other and Rus broke into rival princedoms; Kyiv was even sacked (robbed and burned) by other Rus princes in 1169.
fact: In December 1240 the Mongol army under Batu took Kyiv after a siege as part of a huge campaign across eastern Europe; much of the city was ruined, but it survived as a smaller town.
fact: In western Ukraine the Principality of Galicia-Volhynia was strong in the 13th century; Prince Danylo of Halych received a royal crown from a papal envoy (a messenger of the Pope) in 1253, and Lviv was named after his son Lev, though Danylo still had to accept the Mongols as his overlords (masters).
fact: The Golden Horde dominated most Rus lands from the 1240s; in Ukrainian lands Lithuania pushed it back after the battle of Blue Waters around 1362, so the «300 years» of Mongol rule fits Moscow's lands better than most of Ukraine.
fact: The Mongol empire was destructive in its conquests, but afterwards it kept trade routes safe, ran a postal system and allowed many religions.
exercise: Around which year did Volodymyr accept Christianity? | 988 | 1240 | 1654 | 482
exercise: From where did Volodymyr take Christianity? | Byzantium | Rome and the Pope | The Mongol khans | Sweden and Norway
exercise: How fast did Christianity spread across Rus? | Slowly, over many generations | In one single day, when everyone was baptised in the river | In one week | It never spread at all
exercise: Which religions' envoys does the chronicle say Volodymyr heard? | Islam, Judaism and Western and Byzantine Christianity | Only Byzantine Christianity | Buddhism, Hinduism and the religion of the old Chinese emperors | Only the old pagan gods
exercise: What was the solid reason behind Volodymyr's choice? | An alliance with Byzantium, with trade and a royal marriage | Only the beauty of one church he saw in a dream | A dream about a dragon | An order from the Mongols, who ruled Kyiv at the time and wanted peace
exercise: What was Volodymyr like before his baptism? | A fierce pagan prince who fought his brothers | A quiet monk who never fought anybody in his life | A Mongol khan who rode in from the far eastern steppe | A Cossack hetman who led the Zaporizhian Sich
exercise: When did Yaroslav the Wise rule Kyiv? | 1019–1054 | 945–972 | 1240–1362 | 1648–1657
exercise: Who became Queen of France in 1051? | Anna, Yaroslav's daughter | Olha, the widow of Prince Ihor | Roksolana, born in Rohatyn | Lybid, the sister of Kyi and Shchek
exercise: What survives to show Anna could write? | Her signature in Cyrillic on a French document | A long diary she wrote in English about life at the French court | A statue saying so | A painting by Repin
exercise: What did the Ruska Pravda change? | It replaced blood revenge with fines for many crimes | It banned all trade with Byzantium and closed the roads to the south | It made the Mongols leave | It made Lviv the capital
exercise: What weakened Rus after Yaroslav's death? | His heirs fought each other and the state split | Rus became too rich to defend, so its soldiers stopped training | Everyone moved to Canada | A volcano erupted near Kyiv
exercise: Who took Kyiv in December 1240? | The Mongol army under Batu | The Polish king and his knights | The Swedish Vikings from the north | The Ottoman sultan and his army
exercise: Where was Danylo of Halych's kingdom? | In Galicia-Volhynia, western Ukraine | In Crimea, on the southern coast by the Black Sea | Around Moscow, in the forests of the north-east | In Hungary, across the Carpathian mountains
exercise: Which city was named after Danylo's son Lev? | Lviv | Kharkiv in the east | Odesa on the sea | Lutsk in Volhynia
exercise: Who pushed the Golden Horde out of most Ukrainian lands around 1362? | Lithuania, after the battle of Blue Waters | The Cossacks, after their great victory at Khotyn | Moscow, after Poltava | Sweden, after Hastings
exercise: What good thing did the Mongol empire do after its conquests? | Kept trade routes safe and ran a postal system | Built the first university in Kyiv | Gave every town Magdeburg rights | Ended all wars in Europe
test: teacher writes «Russia was baptised in 988», Danylo writes «Ukraine was». What would a careful historian write? | Rus was baptised in Kyiv, the shared ancestor of both | Only Russia, Kyiv was not involved | Only Ukraine, Russia did not exist yet so nothing happened | Neither, it was in 1240
test: Danylo says Volodymyr dumped Islam because no drinking allowed lol. What's the wise take? | That is a chronicle story; the solid reason was the alliance with Byzantium | That is the proven and only reason | He chose Islam in the end | He never chose any religion
test: A ruler did terrible things young and good things old. How should a history book tell it? | It should keep both halves of his life | It should keep only the good half, so that children have a hero | It should keep only the bad half | It should leave him out
test: Danylo says Yaroslav was father-in-law of ALL Europe. What's fair? | Too much, but three daughters married kings, which is remarkable | True, every single king in Europe married one of his many daughters | False, he had no children at all | He married the Pope's sister
test: Story goes Anna could write and the French king couldn't even sign. What's checked and what isn't? | Her Cyrillic signature is real; the king not reading is not proven | Both are proven facts | Neither is true, Anna never went to France | The king wrote in Cyrillic too
test: Danylo says Ukraine had the first law book in Europe. What's the honest wording? | One of the earliest Slavic law codes, though Roman law was older | The first laws ever written anywhere on Earth, older than Rome or Babylon | There were no laws in Rus | Ruska Pravda was written in 1991
test: Danylo goes «Kyiv fell only cos of enemies». What's missing? | Quarrels among its own princes, who even sacked Kyiv in 1169 | Nothing, only enemies mattered | A great flood in 1169 | An earthquake that split the city
test: What would happen to a country if its rulers fought each other for decades? | It would grow weaker and easier for enemies to attack | It would grow stronger every year | Nothing would change | Enemies would be too scared to come
test: The teacher says the Mongols wiped Kyiv off the map in 1240. What's more exact? | Kyiv was badly damaged and much reduced, but it survived | Kyiv disappeared from the map and was never rebuilt by anyone | The Mongols never reached Kyiv | The Mongols made Kyiv their capital
test: Danylo says Mongols ruled Ukraine 300 years. What's the fix? | In most Ukrainian lands it was about a century; 300 fits Moscow's lands better | He's right for every region | The Mongols ruled for 3 years | The Mongols never ruled anywhere in Rus
test: mate says Mongols only smashed stuff, built nothing. Fair? | No, they also kept trade safe and ran a postal system, though conquest was cruel | Yes, they did nothing else at all but burn, smash and rob every town they ever took | No, they were always gentle | No, they invented the internet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conversion of Rus to Christianity under Prince Volodymyr? | It was initiated circa nine hundred and eighty-eight and was completed only over subsequent generations | It was completed in its entirety within a single day | It was imposed by the Mongols in twelve hundred and forty | It took place in Rome under the Pope
exam: Identify the principal provision by which the Ruska Pravda is distinguished. | The substitution of monetary fines for blood revenge in respect of many offences | The abolition of all monarchy in Rus | The establishment of the Zaporizhian Sich upon the islands of the lower Dnipro River | The prohibition of trade with Byzantium
exam: Ascertain the internal factor which contributed to the decline of Kyivan Rus prior to the Mongol invasion. | Protracted conflict among the princes descended from Yaroslav | An excessive quantity of law codes | The emigration of the populace to Canada | The conversion of the princes and their courts to Islam in large numbers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the condition of Kyiv subsequent to the siege of twelve hundred and forty. | The city was gravely damaged and reduced but continued to exist | The city was entirely obliterated and never inhabited again | The city was spared and enlarged by the Mongols | The city was transferred to the Kingdom of France
